  },
];

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'Which course after BA gives the best salary in India?',
    a: 'By ceiling alone, an MBA from a top institute (IIM, FMS, JBIMS) or a specialised LLM from a top NLU via CLAT-PG typically out-earns the other paths on this list over a 10-year horizon, with senior professionals in both crossing Rs 30-50 LPA-plus. But that ceiling only matters if you can clear CAT or CLAT-PG, or afford the fees at a strong institute without an unmanageable loan. The realistic best course for you depends on your exam stamina, your financial runway, and how fast you need income, not the single highest number on paper.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is MBA a good option for BA graduates?',
    a: 'Yes, and BA graduates are eligible for every major MBA entrance exam (CAT, XAT, CMAT) with no restriction on undergraduate subject. The outcome depends almost entirely on the institute. Top IIMs and FMS Delhi average Rs 22-36 LPA, while a weak or unranked institute on a loan often nets Rs 3.5-6 LPA, a genuinely risky trade if you cannot clear a strong entrance score.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is CLAT-PG and how does it help a BA graduate?',
    a: 'CLAT-PG is the entrance exam for LLM seats at National Law Universities, but it requires an LLB first, so a BA graduate typically completes a 3-year LLB before attempting it. LLM fees at NLUs commonly range from Rs 3,000 to Rs 3 lakh, take about a year, and lead to specialised roles in IP, tax, or corporate law paying Rs 6-20 LPA, considerably more than a generalist LLB alone.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is MA Economics worth it after BA?',
    a: 'It is one of the strongest MA options if you can get into a top program. DSE reports salary packages between Rs 7-22 LPA with an average near Rs 11 LPA, and JNU\'s highest reported off-campus placement has touched roughly Rs 33 LPA. The same MA Economics from a general, lower-ranked university typically starts closer to Rs 4-6 LPA, so the specific college matters more than the degree title.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can a BA graduate get into data analytics without a maths or computer science background?',
