---

<BlogPostLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  category="college-degrees"
  publishedAt={publishedDateISO}
  publishedLabel={publishedDateLabel}
  authorName={authorName}
  authorUrl={authorUrl}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  jsonLdExtras={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>The best courses after BA for good salary in India are MBA, law through a 3-year LLB followed
    by CLAT-PG for an LLM, MA Economics, MA Public Policy, a PG diploma in journalism and mass
    communication, and skill-first routes in digital marketing, data analytics, and UX/UI design, and
    none of them is automatically the right one for you.</strong>
    Each course trades cost, time, and entrance-exam odds for a different salary ceiling, and the course
    with the loudest headline number is not always the one with the best realistic return for your own
    situation. This article ranks all nine paths by real fees, real duration, and real starting-to-mid-career
    salary data, so you can build the <strong>high-value skill portfolio</strong> that actually moves you
    toward <strong>high income opportunities</strong> and <strong>earlier financial freedom</strong>, instead
    of picking a course because it sounded safe or impressive.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>MBA from a strong institute (IIM, FMS, JBIMS) and a specialised LLM via CLAT-PG have the highest realistic ceilings, both crossing Rs 30-50 LPA-plus over 8-10 years, but both carry a real entrance-exam or institute-selection risk.</li>
      <li>MA Economics from DSE or JNU is a genuinely strong, lower-cost route, averaging around Rs 11 LPA at DSE, but the same degree from a lower-ranked college rarely clears Rs 5-6 LPA on its own.</li>
      <li>MA Public Policy and journalism/mass communication diplomas pay modestly at entry (Rs 3-8 LPA) and need a deliberate pivot into consulting, digital strategy, or leadership to grow further.</li>
      <li>Digital marketing, data analytics, and UX/UI design certifications need no entrance exam, no specific BA subject, and start paying inside months, though their ceiling is lower unless you stack a second skill on top.</li>
      <li><strong>The strongest long-term move is rarely one course alone. It is a base credential stacked with one visible, provable skill, because that stack is what turns a job title into a real income ceiling.</strong></li>
      <li>Test your fit with one small proof step this month, a mock CAT or CLAT-PG paper, a small dashboard, or a UX case study, before you commit years or a loan to any single course.</li>
      <li>Humanities domain depth becomes more valuable with AI research tools, strong language and communication, people judgment, and visible work. That stack can open consulting, content, training, research, or independent service paths beyond the first job.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    For the wider picture of every path after BA, not just the highest-salary ones, start with
    <a href="/blog/career-options/career-options-in-arts/">career options in arts</a>. If pay ranked by job
    title is what you need instead of courses, read
    <a href="/blog/career-options/best-paying-jobs-for-humanities-students-india/">best paying jobs for humanities students India</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you want a clearer read on which of these paths genuinely fits your strengths before you spend
    money on coaching or an entrance exam, a session with
    <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/">structured career guidance</a>
    can turn this ranking into a plan built around your actual runway and timeline.
  </p>

  <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="Article sections">
    <p class="jump-nav__label">Jump to section</p>
    <ul class="jump-nav__list">
      {jumpLinks.map((item) => (
        <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.label}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="answer">The honest answer before the list</h2>

  <p>
    Ask most families which course pays the most after BA and you get one of two answers: MBA, or "just
    prepare for UPSC."
  </p>

  <p>
    Neither answer is wrong. Both are incomplete, and the missing part is the part that actually decides
    your outcome.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--amber">
    <p class="callout__eyebrow">The usual bad framing</p>
    <ul class="callout__list">
      <li>BA only really leads to UPSC or a teaching job, so pick one of those and stop looking.</li>
      <li>An MBA is always a safe bet if you can afford the fees.</li>
      <li>MA in a humanities subject does not pay, so switch fields entirely.</li>
      <li>Certifications in analytics, marketing, or design "do not count" as real postgraduate courses.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="callout__footer">
      None of this holds up once you compare the actual fees, actual duration, and actual entrance-exam
      odds of each course against each other, instead of judging them one at a time in isolation.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    A course that pays Rs 30 LPA at year ten is not automatically better than a course that pays Rs 7 LPA
    at year one, if the first course needs a top-3 institute seat that most applicants never get.
  </p>

  <p>
    The real question is not which course sounds the most respectable at a family function. It is which
    one gives you the strongest realistic return for your own entrance-exam readiness, your own financial
    runway, and how fast you genuinely need to start earning.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ranking">Best courses after BA, ranked by real ROI</h2>

  <p>
    Ranked by real cost-to-income math, not brand perception. Compare all nine before you shortlist
    anything.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Course</th>
          <th>Real cost</th>
          <th>Time and entry</th>
          <th>Realistic salary range</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {roiRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Course"><strong>{item.course}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Real cost">{item.cost}</td>
            <td data-label="Time and entry">{item.time}</td>
            <td data-label="Realistic salary range">{item.salary}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Figures are directional, based on current institute fee schedules, official admission-body notices,
    and current salary-tracking sources at the time of writing. Always verify current fees, cutoffs, and
    placement data on the official institute or exam-body website before committing money.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      Notice the pattern: the courses with the highest ceiling (MBA, LLM via CLAT-PG, DSE-tier MA
      Economics) also carry either a tough entrance exam or a real institute-quality risk. The courses
      with the fastest, cheapest entry (digital marketing, data analytics, UX design) top out lower unless
      you stack a second skill on top.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    Before you shortlist anything on this table, run it against a strict planning check: a common heuristic
    is to keep any single course's fee within roughly 10% of your total education budget, and only go
    higher when you can point to specific, verifiable evidence, a placement report, accreditation, faculty
    or lab access, not the institute's brand name alone. On this list, that evidence genuinely exists for
    IIM, FMS Delhi, DSE, and top NLU LLM programs. It usually does not exist for an unranked private MBA or
    law college charging similar or higher fees on a loan.
  </p>

  <h2 id="mba">1. MBA: the institute decides everything</h2>

  <p>
    No course on this list has a wider gap between its best and worst version than MBA, and BA graduates
    are eligible for every major entrance exam with no restriction on undergraduate subject.
  </p>

  <p>
    IIM fees for a recent batch range from roughly Rs 17 lakh to Rs 27 lakh, with average placement
    packages at the top IIMs running Rs 22-36 LPA. FMS Delhi and Delhi School of Economics offer a very
    different trade: fees under Rs 2.5 lakh for the entire program, government-subsidised, with FMS
    reporting average placements around Rs 30-34 LPA, among the highest ROI figures of any MBA program in
    the country precisely because the fee is so low relative to the outcome.
  </p>

  <div class="work-style-grid">
    {mbaCards.map((item) => (
      <div class="work-style-card">
        <div class="work-style-card__label">{item.label}</div>
        <div class="work-style-card__title">{item.title}</div>
        <p>{item.body}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    A weak or unranked-institute MBA taken on an education loan is the single most common expensive
    mistake in this entire decision. Fresher pay from such institutes commonly lands around Rs 3.5-6 LPA,
    which rarely clears loan EMIs within the first few years. Check the official CAT or XAT cutoff and
    the specific institute's placement report before applying.
  </p>

  <h2 id="law">2. Law: 3-year LLB, then CLAT-PG for an LLM</h2>

  <p>
    Law is the path most guides skip when talking about BA graduates, because CLAT itself is usually
    associated with 5-year integrated courses for 12th-pass students. A BA graduate's actual route runs
    through a 3-year LLB first.
  </p>

  <p>
    Government law colleges charge roughly Rs 10,000-50,000 a year, and private colleges commonly run
    Rs 1-5 lakh a year, with entry now largely through CUET PG or a university's own law entrance test.
    Corporate law is one of the higher-paying entry fields once qualified: freshers can expect roughly
    Rs 40,000-80,000 a month at a strong firm, translating to Rs 4-10 LPA depending on the employer and
    city.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      A generalist 3-year LLB caps out faster than most people expect. The real ceiling on this path opens
      up through CLAT-PG, the entrance exam for LLM seats at National Law Universities, which you can only
      attempt after finishing your LLB. LLM fees at NLUs range roughly Rs 3,000-3 lakh, take about a year,
      and lead to specialised roles in IP, tax, or corporate and tech law paying Rs 6-20 LPA, a real step up
      from a generalist law degree alone.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Verify current CLAT-PG eligibility, dates, and participating NLUs on the
    <a href="https://consortiumofnlus.ac.in/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">official CLAT Consortium of NLUs portal</a>
    before planning your LLB-to-LLM timeline.
  </p>

  <h2 id="economics">3. MA Economics: the quant-heavy route</h2>

  <p>
    MA Economics is one of the most polarising options on this list. Done at the right institute, it is a
    genuinely high-paying route. Done anywhere else, it plateaus quickly.
  </p>

  <p>
    Delhi School of Economics reports salary packages between Rs 7-22 LPA with an average around Rs 11
    LPA, placing students into business analysis, investment banking, and economic research roles at
    firms like Goldman Sachs, American Express, and Nomura. JNU blends the same mathematical training with
    development-economics and policy work, and its highest reported off-campus placement has touched
    roughly Rs 33 LPA.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      The same MA Economics degree from a general, lower-ranked university typically starts closer to
      Rs 4-6 LPA. The subject is not what commands the premium here. The specific college, and the
      quantitative rigour it forces on you, is what does.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="public-policy">4. MA Public Policy: the newest high-value MA</h2>

  <p>
    Public policy is a relatively recent addition to India's postgraduate landscape, and most BA graduates
    still do not know it exists as a distinct, well-funded option beyond a generic MA in Political
    Science.
  </p>

  <p>
    TISS Hyderabad's public policy program costs roughly Rs 1.68 lakh for the full two years, while
    Jindal School of Government and Public Policy runs closer to Rs 4 lakh a year. Entry-level salaries
    across these programs start around Rs 6-8 LPA in policy analysis, research, and CSR roles, rising to
    Rs 15-20 LPA with experience and international exposure at multilateral organisations or large
    consulting firms.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      Public policy pays less at entry than a top MBA or a strong MA Economics, but it is one of the few
      routes on this list where a genuine argument-construction and writing background from a BA in
      humanities is a direct advantage, not a gap to overcome.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="journalism">5. PG Diploma in Journalism and Mass Communication</h2>

  <p>
    Journalism training is often the first course a BA graduate considers, and also the one with the
    widest gap between its average and its best-case outcome.
  </p>

  <p>
    IIMC's PG diploma fees run roughly Rs 62,000-2 lakh for the year, depending on the specialisation
    (English or Hindi journalism, radio and TV, or advertising and PR). The average reported placement
    package is only Rs 3-4 LPA, though the highest reported package has touched Rs 20 LPA, a gap almost
    entirely explained by which newsroom or media house a graduate lands in, and whether they pivot into
    digital strategy or brand-side communication.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      Pure reporting pay stays modest for years without a deliberate move. The graduates who clear Rs 8-15
      LPA within a few years are almost always the ones who pivoted into digital editorial leadership,
      brand journalism, or media-business roles, not the ones who stayed purely on the reporting beat.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="digital-marketing">6. Digital marketing certification</h2>

  <p>
    If a multi-year postgraduate degree does not fit your timeline or budget, this is the fastest-moving
    entry track for a BA graduate right now, and it needs no specific subject background.
  </p>

  <p>
    Job-ready digital marketing certifications cost roughly Rs 40,000-1,00,000 and run 3-8 months, with
    IIT-affiliated PG certificate programs running up to Rs 2 lakh for a more structured six-month track.
    In-house freshers typically start around Rs 3.5-6 LPA, rising to Rs 6-12 LPA within 2-4 years.
    Performance-marketing and marketing-analytics specialists command the strongest packages on this
    route, with some senior roles exceeding Rs 25-30 LPA.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      Nobody hires you for a certificate name in digital marketing. They hire you for one finished
      campaign you can walk them through, with real spend, reach, and conversion numbers attached. Build
      that proof before you apply for roles, not after.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="data-analytics">7. Data analytics for non-STEM graduates</h2>

  <p>
    This is the route BA graduates most often assume is closed to them, and it is not. Data analytics
    certification programs are specifically designed for non-technical entrants, and in India, analytics
    roles are commonly filled by commerce graduates, MBAs, economics students, and social-science
    graduates, not only engineers.
  </p>

  <p>
    Advanced certificate programs cost roughly Rs 30,000-1,80,000 and run 3-9 months, while a full
    post-graduate diploma can run up to Rs 6 lakh over 6-12 months. Entry salaries typically start around
    Rs 4-6.5 LPA, rising to Rs 8-15 LPA within 3-5 years, and to Rs 20-25 LPA-plus in analytics leadership
    roles over a longer horizon.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      The barrier here is psychological, not technical. Business analytics is closer to structured
      reasoning and communication than to advanced mathematics, which is exactly the skill set a strong
      BA education already builds.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="ux-design">8. UX/UI design bootcamp</h2>

  <p>
    UX/UI design is one of the more genuine career pivots for BA graduates precisely because hiring in
    this field is built around a portfolio, not a specific undergraduate degree.
  </p>

  <p>
    Bootcamp fees typically run Rs 25,000-1.5 lakh over 4-6 months, and hiring managers care far more
    about 3-5 strong case studies showing real user research and design decisions than about which subject
    you studied. Fresh bootcamp graduates commonly start around Rs 3-7 LPA, with entry-level roles at
    genuine product companies reaching Rs 6-15 LPA once a strong portfolio is in place, and experienced
    product designers earning Rs 20-35 LPA or more.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      A BA in a humanities subject, especially psychology, sociology, or literature, gives you a real head
      start in the user-research half of UX work. The design-tool half is learnable in months. Do not let
      the word "design" scare you off this path before checking what the actual daily work looks like.
    </p>
  </div>

